README-FORTRAN -- the RTF/68K Fortran-77 system, and the one thing it needs==========================================================================This disk carries a complete Fortran-77 compiler.  It went unnoticed for along time because every part of it appeared to do nothing at all: run `rtf`,`for`, `lnk`, `biory` or `creadoc` and you get silence and a prompt back.They are not broken.  Every one of them calls F$Link for a module named`os9lib`, gets E_MNF (module not found), and exits without printing anything.  $ /dd/CMDS/for  F$Link  A0="os9lib"  F$Link  returns #000:221 - E_MNF  F$Exit  D1.w=$00DD          <- 221, the same error, as the exit statusWHAT os9lib IS--------------`CMDS/os9lib` is the RTF Fortran RUN-TIME LIBRARY, not a program.  DOC/INDEXdescribed it as a support library all along, but a sweep that ran every filein CMDS as a program ran this one too, hit the floating-point code inside it,and recorded "wants a 68881".  Running a library as a program proves nothing.MAKING IT WORK--------------The module has to be in the module directory before anything can link it.OS-9's own `load` command is Microware's and is not on this disk, so themodule directory has to be supplied when the emulator starts:  OS9MDIR=<host directory holding os9lib> os9exec ...With that set, all five run:  for       links os9lib and returns cleanly  rtf       " RTF/68K Version 2.14 (19-May-1987) "  biory     asks for a name and computes a biorhythm (German prompts)  creadoc   pulls documentation comments out of Fortran source  lnk       runs, and then wants a file this disk does not have -- see belowHOW FAR YOU CAN GET-------------------  COMPILING WORKS.  `rtf /h6/prime.f` on the demo in SRC/rtf reports    " Total Errors   0   Warnings   0 / RTF normally completed "  and writes `prime.a`.  ASSEMBLING AND LINKING DO NOT.  rtf emits 68000 ASSEMBLY, not an object  file.  Turning that into a module needs Microware's `r68` and `l68`, and  `lnk` calls l68 with `/h0/LIB/sys.l` -- Microware's library.  Neither is  here, and neither is covered by the permission this collection holds.  The five .as0/as1/as4/as5/as11 assemblers on this disk are 6800, 6801,  6804, 6805 and 68HC11 cross-assemblers and cannot assemble 68k code.  So: a working Fortran-77 front end, and two precompiled example programs  that run.  Completing the chain needs Microware's assembler and linker.WHAT IS HERE------------  DOC/rtf/rtfman.txt    the RTF/68K manual, 227 KB, compiler and run-time                        library, version 2.13, 12 March 1987  DOC/rtf/biory.doc     documentation creadoc generated from biory.f --  DOC/rtf/creadoc.doc   which is also the demonstration of what creadoc does  SRC/rtf/*.f           biory, creadoc, and five demos: prime, bench,                        float, div, vtest  SRC/rtf/rtfstart.a    the start-up code lnk wants, and its 68020 variant  SRC/rtf/oskdefs.d     OSK definitions for assembling itRTF/68K is a CERN product.  Its manual states the package is distributedfree.  The compiler, its data module, the library and the two examples cameto this collection from EFFO public-domain disk 1 and EFFO forum disk 5.